---
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/CallToAction.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import heroPhoto from '../../assets/communities/derby/derby-hero-city-of-derby.jpg';
import highParkPhoto from '../../assets/communities/derby/derby-high-park-gazebo-city-of-derby.jpg';
import warrenRiverviewPhoto from '../../assets/communities/derby/derby-warren-riverview-park-city-of-derby.jpg';
import rockRiverRapidsPhoto from '../../assets/communities/derby/rock-river-rapids-aerial-rock-river-rapids.jpg';
import dinosaursTrailPhoto from '../../assets/communities/derby/derby-field-station-dinosaurs-kids-field-station-dinosaurs.jpg';
import dinosaursGroupPhoto from '../../assets/communities/derby/field-station-dinosaurs-group-presentation-field-station-dinosaurs.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Derby, Kansas Community Guide | Charity Menefee"
  description="A practical introduction to Derby, Kansas, including its location south of Wichita, housing growth, local parks, and property considerations."
  pageClass="page-interior page-derby"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Community guide · Derby"
    title="A growing city just south of Wichita."
    intro="Derby is closely connected to the Wichita metro while functioning as a substantial city with established neighborhoods, newer growth, commercial corridors, parks, and civic spaces of its own."
    image={heroPhoto}
    imageAlt="The gazebo and footbridge beside the fishing pond at High Park in Derby at sunset"
    variant="community"
    imagePosition="38% center"
    imagePositionMobile="20% center"
  />

  <section class="section-space" aria-labelledby="derby-overview-title">
    <div class="shell editorial-grid">
      <div data-reveal="left">
        <p class="eyebrow">Getting oriented</p>
        <h2 id="derby-overview-title">A city of its own immediately south of Wichita.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose" data-reveal="right">
        <p>Derby is an incorporated city in Sedgwick County, directly south of Wichita. K-15 is an important north and south connection between the two cities, while Rock Road is another major corridor through Derby.</p>
        <p>The physical connection to the larger Wichita metro is close, but Derby functions as a substantial city in its own right. Residential areas, commercial corridors, parks, recreation, and civic spaces extend across the community rather than centering on a single edge near Wichita.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-space section-surface" aria-labelledby="derby-housing-title">
    <div class="shell editorial-grid">
      <div data-reveal="left">
        <p class="eyebrow">Housing and continued growth</p>
        <h2 id="derby-housing-title">Different periods of growth shape Derby's housing.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose" data-reveal="right">
        <p>Derby's housing reflects several decades of expansion, with established residential areas closer to its older street network and later additions extending the city's footprint. That range makes the home's age, lot pattern, and connections to K-15 and Rock Road more useful than a subdivision name alone.</p>
        <p>For a newer home or planned development, review the specific lot, included improvements, restrictions, and any HOA or special-assessment obligations tied to the address. The City's Vision Derby 2040 comprehensive plan addresses that continued growth through land use, transportation, and housing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-space section-sage" aria-labelledby="derby-parks-title">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading split-heading">
        <div data-reveal="left">
          <p class="eyebrow">Local character</p>
          <h2 id="derby-parks-title">Two parks anchor opposite sides of the city.</h2>
        </div>
        <p data-reveal="right">High Park and Warren Riverview Park sit on different edges of Derby and serve different purposes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card-grid reveal-stagger">
        <figure class="feature-card" data-reveal="left-scale">
          <div class="feature-card-media">
            <Picture
              src={highParkPhoto}
              formats={['avif', 'webp']}
              widths={[480, 720, 960]}
              sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 50vw, 100vw"
              alt="The gazebo beside the fishing pond at High Park in Derby"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            <strong>High Park</strong>
            <p>Derby's largest park, on the east side of the city, just finished a renovation that added a new splash pad, playground, and shelter on its south end and an eight-court lighted pickleball complex on its north end. It also includes a fishing pond, amphitheater, gazebo, ballfields, and more than two miles of hike-and-bike path.</p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="feature-card" data-reveal="right-scale">
          <div class="feature-card-media">
            <Picture
              src={warrenRiverviewPhoto}
              formats={['avif', 'webp']}
              widths={[480, 720, 960, 1200]}
              sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 50vw, 100vw"
              alt="An aerial view of Warren Riverview Park's terraced amphitheater and river landing along the Arkansas River"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            <strong>Warren Riverview Park</strong>
            <p>Opened in 2018 on the east bank of the Arkansas River, this four-acre park is a different kind of setting than Derby's more developed corridors. It includes a canoe and kayak launch, a ropes course, an accessible playground, a covered shelter, and a terraced amphitheater and river landing built from Kansas limestone.</p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="photo-banner section-surface" aria-labelledby="derby-rrr-title">
    <div class="photo-banner-media">
      <Picture
        src={rockRiverRapidsPhoto}
        formats={['avif', 'webp']}
        widths={[960, 1440, 1920, 2600]}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt="An aerial view of Rock River Rapids in Derby, showing the lazy river, zero-depth play pool, lap pool, and water slides"
      />
      <p class="photo-credit">© Rock River Rapids</p>
    </div>
    <div class="shell photo-banner-copy" data-reveal="up">
      <p class="eyebrow">A local water park</p>
      <h2 id="derby-rrr-title">The Derby Recreation Commission runs its own water park.</h2>
      <p>Rock River Rapids is a public water park at 1900 E James Street, run by the Derby Recreation Commission rather than the city government directly. It's open seasonally, typically from Memorial Day through late summer.</p>
      <p>The park includes a lazy river, four waterslides, and a lap pool with diving boards and a climbing wall. A separate zero-depth play area, topped by a tipping water bucket, gives younger kids their own space apart from the main slides.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-space section-sage" aria-labelledby="derby-dinosaurs-title">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading split-heading">
        <div data-reveal="left">
          <p class="eyebrow">A one-of-a-kind attraction</p>
          <h2 id="derby-dinosaurs-title">Field Station: Dinosaurs brings a walking trail of life-size models to Derby.</h2>
        </div>
        <p data-reveal="right">More than 40 life-size dinosaur models sit among native prairie grasses along a 10-acre trail, with live presentations and more rounding out the visit.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card-grid reveal-stagger">
        <figure class="feature-card" data-reveal="left-scale">
          <div class="feature-card-media">
            <Picture
              src={dinosaursTrailPhoto}
              formats={['avif', 'webp']}
              widths={[480, 720, 960]}
              sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 50vw, 100vw"
              alt="Two children walking a gravel trail toward large dinosaur models at Field Station: Dinosaurs"
            />
            <p class="photo-credit">© Field Station: Dinosaurs</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            <strong>A walking trail among the dinosaurs</strong>
            <p>More than 40 life-size dinosaur models line a 10-acre trail through native prairie grasses at 2999 N Rock Rd, mixing regional favorites with species discovered in Kansas.</p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="feature-card" data-reveal="right-scale">
          <div class="feature-card-media">
            <Picture
              src={dinosaursGroupPhoto}
              formats={['avif', 'webp']}
              widths={[480, 720, 960]}
              sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 50vw, 100vw"
              alt="A large group of schoolchildren watching a costumed dinosaur presentation at Field Station: Dinosaurs"
            />
            <p class="photo-credit">© Field Station: Dinosaurs</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            <strong>Live shows and more to explore</strong>
            <p>Live educational presentations run throughout a visit, and the Paleo Playground and a mini-golf course give the park more to do beyond the trail. It operates seasonally rather than year-round.</p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-space" aria-labelledby="derby-practical-title">
    <div class="shell editorial-grid">
      <div data-reveal="left">
        <p class="eyebrow">Practical things to know</p>
        <h2 id="derby-practical-title">Start with the home, then look at the routes around it.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose" data-reveal="right">
        <p>Compare whether an established area or newer addition fits the property characteristics you want to maintain. The home's age, materials, lot, and included improvements can matter more than a broad label about the surrounding area.</p>
        <p>Then consider how the address connects to K-15, Rock Road, and the Arkansas River side of the city. That local orientation helps narrow the search before property-specific restrictions or costs are reviewed.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-space section-surface" aria-labelledby="derby-search-title">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading split-heading">
        <div data-reveal="left">
          <p class="eyebrow">Useful questions</p>
          <h2 id="derby-search-title">What deserves a closer look when searching in Derby?</h2>
        </div>
        <p data-reveal="right">The answers depend on the individual home, lot, and development. These questions provide a practical place to begin.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="question-grid reveal-stagger">
        <li data-reveal="up">Would you rather compare an established neighborhood, newer development, or both?</li>
        <li data-reveal="up">Which major roads would you actually use from this location?</li>
        <li data-reveal="up">What maintenance considerations come with the home's age, materials, and current condition?</li>
        <li data-reveal="up">For new construction, what's included and which HOA obligations, assessments, costs, or restrictions need verification?</li>
        <li data-reveal="up">Which lot characteristics could affect use, drainage, upkeep, or future plans?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <nav class="shell next-community" aria-label="Community pages">
    <span>Keep exploring</span>
    <a href="/communities/">All communities <i aria-hidden="true">→</i></a>
  </nav>
  <CallToAction
    title="Have questions about a property in Derby?"
    copy="If you're comparing communities or have questions about a specific property, tell me what you're considering. I’ll help you sort through the location and property details that matter."
  />
</BaseLayout>
